sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch04-23> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "In a fully stocked country, improved descendants of a species tend to supplant their predecessors, so the intermediate forms between earlier and later states, and the original parent species itself, generally tend to become extinct.";
    dc:type "prediction";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Intermediate forms and parent species tend to go extinct";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1st ed., London: John Murray, 1859), Chapter IV, “Natural Selection”; section “Action of Natural Selection, through Divergence of Character and Extinction, on the descendants from a common parent”. Scope: lineages of species descended from a common parent"
}
